From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 21:40:18 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: hold fewer capture buffers for H.264 and HEVC streams

The decoder told the framework to keep 16 frames in flight for an H.264 or
HEVC stream, which the framework turns into 20 capture buffers. At 4K a
capture buffer is 11.87 MiB in the guest and again on the host, so a stream
held 237 MiB on each side. 16 is the deepest reordering a stream can have,
and suits a decoder that keeps the pictures it reorders in its capture
buffers. This device reorders on the host, and its capture buffers hold no
pictures that wait for their display order.

The framework stops queuing work once the works that wait for an output, with
their input released, reach the pipeline delay plus the output delay plus 4,
and the host releases a picture once more than the stream's reorder depth R of
them wait, so a stream stalls only when R exceeds the sum of the two delays
by more than 3. R is at most 16, so a sum of 13 stalls no stream. Only a work
that is done can tell the framework another delay, and a stream that needs the
whole limit completes none before it has it, so the sum is set for every
stream at the start rather than lowered for a stream that reorders little.

The framework sizes the capture buffer queue from the output delay and the
input buffer queue from the pipeline delay, each plus 4. A capture buffer
costs 23.7 MiB at 4K (the guest's and the host's picture), an input block
4 MiB, so the delays are 4 and 9 for H.264 and HEVC instead of 10 and 3:
8 capture buffers, not 14, and 13 input slots, not 7. At 4K that is 142 MiB
less for the pictures and 24 MiB more for the input blocks, and the stall
limit stays the 17 works it was.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 README.md                                     | 18 ++++++++
 components/DecodeInterface.cpp                | 44 +++++++++++++++++--
 .../v4l2_codec2/components/DecodeInterface.h  |  5 +++
 v4l2/V4L2ComponentFactory.cpp                 |  6 ++-
 4 files changed, 68 insertions(+), 5 deletions(-)

diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index 0de91967e2e3e683b29d72285e514072aeec0b49..500f3d1b792abeb79849375de76ecf929b35eadc 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -479,6 +479,24 @@ are still in use against the number of V4L2 buffers, and the decoder lets a free
 V4L2 buffer stand for a block it has not met, since the picture buffer is passed
 again with every queue operation.
 
+The output delay of an H.264 or HEVC stream is 4 frames and its pipeline delay
+is 9. The framework adds 4 of its own to each and sizes the capture buffer queue
+from the output delay and the input buffer queue from the pipeline delay, so a
+4K stream holds 8 capture buffers of 11.87 MiB in the guest and again on the
+host (where the stock decoder's 16 would hold 20) and 13 input slots of 4 MiB.
+The device reorders pictures on the host and its capture buffers hold none that
+wait for their display order, so the delays only keep the framework feeding the
+decoder until the host releases a picture: a stream does not stall while its
+reorder depth is at most the pipeline delay plus the output delay plus 3, which
+is 16, and no H.264 or HEVC stream is deeper than 16. The stall limit is the
+same 17 works that an output delay of 10 with a pipeline delay of 3 gave, bought
+with input blocks instead of capture buffers because a capture buffer costs six
+times as much at 4K. A work that is done is the only way to tell the framework
+another delay, and a stream that needs the whole limit completes none before it
+has it, so the limit is the same for every stream and set at the start. A device
+that keeps the pictures it reorders in its capture buffers needs an output delay
+of 16.
+
 ### Input Blocks
 
 The device takes a coded buffer in a dma-buf at least as large as the buffer
diff --git a/components/DecodeInterface.cpp b/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
index d2b322b00edd8f59c95e25e5cee85338e452a6d7..cb0f121a7bb40513fadd7205e2c320e271d5c27e 100644
--- a/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
+++ b/components/DecodeInterface.cpp
@@ -88,6 +88,28 @@ size_t inputBufferSizeFor(uint32_t codedBufferSize) {
     if (codedBufferSize == 0) return kMaxInputBufferSize;
     return std::clamp<size_t>(codedBufferSize, kMinInputBufferSize, kMaxInputBufferSize);
 }
+
+// The output and pipeline delays of H.264 and HEVC, in frames. The framework sizes the capture
+// buffer queue from the output delay and the input buffer queue from the pipeline delay (and the
+// input delay, which is 0), each plus 4 of its own for smooth playback. A 4K picture is 11.87 MiB
+// in the guest and again on the host, so a capture buffer costs 23.7 MiB at 4K, where an input
+// block costs 4 MiB.
+//
+// A decoder that keeps the pictures it reorders in its capture buffers needs 16, the deepest
+// reordering a stream can have, as its output delay. This device reorders on the host, and its
+// capture buffers hold no pictures that wait for their display order, so the delay only has to
+// keep the framework feeding the decoder until the host releases a picture. The framework stops
+// queuing work once the works that wait for an output, with their input already released, reach
+// the pipeline delay plus the output delay plus 4, and the host releases a picture once more than
+// the stream's reorder depth R of them wait, so it needs R + 1 works queued: no stream stalls
+// while R <= the pipeline delay + the output delay + 3. R is at most 16 (the HEVC limit,
+// sps_max_num_reorder_pics, is 15; an H.264 stream without a bitstream restriction can reach 16),
+// which a sum of 13 covers. Only a work that is done can tell the framework another delay, and a
+// stream that needs the whole limit completes none before it has it, so the limit is the same for
+// every stream and set at the start. It is bought where it is cheapest: an output delay of 4 (8
+// capture buffers with the framework's) and a pipeline delay of 9.
+constexpr uint32_t kReorderingOutputDelay = 4;
+constexpr uint32_t kReorderingPipelineDelay = 9;
 }  // namespace
 
 // static
@@ -403,9 +425,11 @@ DecodeInterface::DecodeInterface(const std::string& name,
     // being allocated but rarely used. This lets us avoid remapping v4l2 input buffers and DMA
     // buffers in the common case. We could go up to 4 here, to limit the framework to
     // simultaneously enqueuing 16 input buffers, but there doesn't seem to be much of an a
-    // performance improvement from that.
+    // performance improvement from that. H.264 and HEVC take more, which with their output delay
+    // gives the stall limit of kReorderingPipelineDelay: at most 13 inputs wait for the device,
+    // which has 16 buffers for them.
     addParameter(DefineParam(mPipelineDelay, C2_PARAMKEY_PIPELINE_DELAY)
-                         .withConstValue(new C2PipelineDelayTuning(3))
+                         .withConstValue(new C2PipelineDelayTuning(getPipelineDelay(*mVideoCodec)))
                          .build());
 
     addParameter(DefineParam(mInputMediaType, C2_PARAMKEY_INPUT_MEDIA_TYPE)
@@ -626,6 +650,18 @@ c2_status_t DecodeInterface::queryColorAspects(
     return status;
 }
 
+uint32_t DecodeInterface::getPipelineDelay(VideoCodec codec) {
+    switch (codec) {
+    case VideoCodec::H264:
+    case VideoCodec::HEVC:
+        return kReorderingPipelineDelay;
+    case VideoCodec::VP8:
+    case VideoCodec::VP9:
+    case VideoCodec::AV1:
+        return kBasePipelineDelay;
+    }
+}
+
 uint32_t DecodeInterface::getOutputDelay(VideoCodec codec) {
     switch (codec) {
     case VideoCodec::H264:
@@ -633,9 +669,9 @@ uint32_t DecodeInterface::getOutputDelay(VideoCodec codec) {
         // queued before being able to output the associated decoded buffers. We need to tell the
         // codec2 framework that it should not stop queuing new work items until the maximum number
         // of frame reordering is reached, to avoid stalling the decoder.
-        return 16;
+        return kReorderingOutputDelay;
     case VideoCodec::HEVC:
-        return 16;
+        return kReorderingOutputDelay;
     case VideoCodec::VP8:
         // The decoder might held a few frames as a reference for decoding. Since Android T
         // the Codec2 is more prone to timeout the component if one is not producing frames. This
diff --git a/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/DecodeInterface.h b/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/DecodeInterface.h
index 338b4b10746981103b7763ff0c431ac48e33387d..8fbe1362876ee62760370cab1109e15b5e87c3ee 100644
--- a/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/DecodeInterface.h
+++ b/components/include/v4l2_codec2/components/DecodeInterface.h
@@ -44,6 +44,11 @@ public:
     HalPixelFormat getOutputPixelFormat() const;
 
     static uint32_t getOutputDelay(VideoCodec codec);
+    // The pipeline delay of a codec: the frames the framework keeps in flight besides the output
+    // delay and its own 4. kBasePipelineDelay unless the codec reorders in the device (H.264 and
+    // HEVC), where the stall limit is the sum of the two.
+    static constexpr uint32_t kBasePipelineDelay = 3;
+    static uint32_t getPipelineDelay(VideoCodec codec);
 
     // The size of the coded buffers the decoder asks the device for, which an input block is at
     // least as large as when it is queued: the maximum input size, which follows the picture size
diff --git a/v4l2/V4L2ComponentFactory.cpp b/v4l2/V4L2ComponentFactory.cpp
index 4493a22daeef46d526e65c1814a2c8206720fac3..04e67a945bf9d58d314d55d07ac2e1bf4829b7b7 100644
--- a/v4l2/V4L2ComponentFactory.cpp
+++ b/v4l2/V4L2ComponentFactory.cpp
@@ -54,7 +54,11 @@ V4L2ComponentFactory::V4L2ComponentFactory(const std::string& componentName, boo
     constexpr nsecs_t kMinFrameIntervalNs = 1000000000ull / 60;
     uint32_t delayCount = 0;
     for (auto c : kAllCodecs) {
-        delayCount = std::max(delayCount, DecodeInterface::getOutputDelay(c));
+        // The frames a decoder can hold back: the output delay and the pipeline delay beyond the
+        // base one, which the interval has always been based on.
+        delayCount = std::max(delayCount, DecodeInterface::getOutputDelay(c) +
+                                                  DecodeInterface::getPipelineDelay(c) -
+                                                  DecodeInterface::kBasePipelineDelay);
     }
     utils::InputBufferManager::setNotificationInterval(delayCount * kMinFrameIntervalNs / 2);
 }
